From 3bbd05ba8a6f772adda1b048845386f8da5800ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:10:15 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0096 -- uclock-backed engine Clock and a
 deadline frame pacer

Two halves of one fix; either alone makes things worse.

THE CLOCK. On DJGPP every std::chrono clock is gettimeofday() (libstdc++
here has _GLIBCXX_USE_GETTIMEOFDAY and no _GLIBCXX_USE_CLOCK_MONOTONIC),
and gettimeofday() is two real-mode INT 21h round trips plus mktime():
1324.6 us per read, 55 ms resolution, measured on a 486DX-66. The engine
read it ~4.7 times per game tick -- 6.3 ms of a 25 ms tick spent asking
the time, for an answer good to two frames. util/time_util.h now aliases
Clock to DosUclockClock under __DJGPP__ (every other platform keeps
upstream's alias untouched): uclock()-backed, 4.9 us per read, ~1 us
resolution.

uclock()'s limits were read from libc's own uclock.o, not assumed:
 - it can step BACKWARDS by one BIOS tick (~55 ms) when the PIT counter
   has reloaded but IRQ0 is not yet serviced -- already seen here as
   tickstats clamped_neg. now() therefore never returns less than it
   last returned; is_steady = true is honest only because of that guard.
 - midnight is handled inside uclock() (one rollover per call pair).
 - its first call reprograms PIT channel 0 to mode 2 and blocks <= 55 ms
   for a tick edge; this port's instrumentation has done that since 0079.
duration stays std::chrono::nanoseconds, the type steady_clock had, so
no other line of the engine changes type; the conversion is split into
seconds + remainder because t * 1e9 overflows int64 after ~2 hours.
Nothing converts a Clock::time_point to calendar time (that is all on
ScriptDateTime::SystemClock = system_clock), and the one
time_point kept in game state is not serialized.

FastClock too. It is aliased to system_clock upstream, which here is
the same gettimeofday(); ccInstance::Run() reads it on every script
function run (the script-timeout watchdog), so every rep_exec call of
every tick paid 1.3 ms for it. Its users only ever subtract two
readings (that watchdog, and game.cpp's load stopwatches), so under
__DJGPP__ FastClock is now Clock. (Added after the first cells of the
ABBA: the zone-bug cells had shown rep_exec_wrapper and
process_events_dispatch scaling with clock cost, which is what gave
this away. Those cells are void for the B arm and were re-run.)

THE PACER. WaitForNextFrame() ended in std::this_thread::sleep_for(),
which on DJGPP is usleep() spinning on the 55 ms clock(): 54.8 ms for a
1, 10 or 25 ms request alike. With the old coarse clock that rarely
fired; with an accurate clock it would fire every frame and cap the
game at ~18 fps -- hence one patch. It is now a deadline wait on the new
Clock. WaitForNextFrame() is the only pacer in the engine (fades,
ShakeScreen, dialog options, inventory, Display, csci dialogs all call
it), so this fixes every per-frame wait at once. Left alone, on
purpose: platform->Delay() (one 500 ms splash hold; the rest
unreachable on DOS), YieldCPU() (suspend loops that never run on DOS),
and video.cpp's sleep_for(8 ms) -- per-frame, but only during video
playback, which no fixture exercises, so a change could not be tested.

It is a busy-wait, and this hub has shipped a bad one (it starved the
BIOS timer, the clock ran slow, self-reported fps went UP while real
speed collapsed). This one keeps interrupts ENABLED throughout -- no
cli, no PIC mask, only PIT channel 0 reads and the audio poll -- so IRQ0
is serviced as everywhere else, and patch 0095 checks uclock() against
the CMOS RTC over the whole run rather than trusting that. It does NOT
use __dpmi_yield(): 123 us on the 486, but 54.6 ms under DOSBox-X,
which would rebuild the cap on the emulator the correctness gates use.

audio_core_entry_poll() keeps running inside the wait, about once a
millisecond, so a sound-on run is pumped more often than before. A poll
only starts if the wait has room for it, judged by what recent polls
cost: the first DOSBox-X run used a fixed 2 ms margin, and an 11 ms
poll (DOSBox-X; ~1 ms on the 486) ran through the deadline -- max wait
32 ms. With the adaptive margin the max wait is 25 ms.

THE SAMPLERS. end_of_tick's two once-a-second samplers each read the
clock every tick on top of game_loop_update_fps()'s own read; all three
now share one. They stay time-gated rather than tick-counted: fps_p50
is a percentile over once-per-real-second samples, and gating on ticks
would make it tick-weighted and incomparable with every recorded
figure.

DOSBox-X: Trilby's Notes screenshot byte-identical (sha256
03de57f79303d68c...), fps_p50 40.04, stall-free window 811 ticks in
20.275 s = 40.00 ticks/s, tick_rate_steady 40.43 (the 0.4 over is the
fixed-timestep loop repaying 5 ticks after each of two startup stalls,
as before this patch: 40.47); AGS Quest AutoPilot all phases ok.
---
 Engine/ac/timer.cpp      | 53 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 Engine/main/game_run.cpp | 27 +++++++++++---
 Engine/util/time_util.h  | 77 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 153 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Engine/ac/timer.cpp b/Engine/ac/timer.cpp
index 6a6ea79fc..feaaa4f3a 100644
--- a/Engine/ac/timer.cpp
+++ b/Engine/ac/timer.cpp
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include "SDL.h"
 #endif
 #ifdef __DJGPP__
+#include <algorithm> // std::max -- DOS-PORT patch 0096
 #include <time.h> // uclock_t/uclock()/UCLOCKS_PER_SEC -- DOS-PORT patch 0081
 #include "main/dos_port_tick_timing.h" // DOS-PORT patch 0081
 #endif
@@ -121,6 +122,58 @@ void WaitForNextFrame()
 #if AGS_PLATFORM_OS_EMSCRIPTEN
         // pass the time as negative in Emscripten Platform Driver
         platform->Delay(-ToMilliseconds(frame_time_remaining));
+#elif defined(__DJGPP__)
+        // DOS-PORT patch 0096: a deadline wait on the uclock-backed Clock
+        // instead of sleep_for(). On DJGPP sleep_for() is usleep(), which
+        // spins on clock() -- 55 ms BIOS-tick resolution -- so ANY positive
+        // request costs a whole tick: 54.8 ms measured for 1, 10 and 25 ms
+        // alike on a 486DX-66. A frame that finished with 5 ms to spare
+        // paid 55 for it, which caps a paced loop at ~18 fps no matter how
+        // cheap the frame is.
+        //
+        // This is a busy-wait, and this hub has shipped a bad one before
+        // (it starved the BIOS timer, the clock ran slow, and the
+        // self-reported fps went UP while real speed collapsed). What is
+        // different here: interrupts stay ENABLED throughout -- no cli, no
+        // PIC mask write, nothing but reads of PIT channel 0 (inside
+        // uclock()) and the audio poll -- so IRQ0 is serviced exactly as it
+        // is anywhere else in the engine, and the clock this loop watches
+        // keeps advancing. And it is checked rather than trusted: patch
+        // 0095 measures uclock() against the CMOS RTC over the whole run.
+        //
+        // Deliberately NOT __dpmi_yield(): 123 us per call on the 486 under
+        // CWSDPMI, but 54.6 ms per call under DOSBox-X (it halts until the
+        // next timer interrupt there) -- it would rebuild the 18 fps cap
+        // on the emulator every correctness gate runs on.
+        //
+        // Audio keeps being pumped DURING the wait, about once a
+        // millisecond -- more often than the single poll per frame it got
+        // before, so a sound-on run is fed better, not starved. A poll is
+        // only started when the wait has room for it: what the last polls
+        // cost (slowly decaying maximum) plus 1 ms. That cost is not a
+        // constant -- ~1 ms on the 486 with Sound Blaster PCM, but 11 ms
+        // per call under DOSBox-X -- and a fixed margin let a poll run
+        // straight through the deadline there.
+        {
+            static Clock::duration poll_cost = std::chrono::milliseconds(2);
+            const auto deadline = next_frame_timestamp;
+            auto next_poll = now + std::chrono::milliseconds(1); // the entry poll above already ran
+            for (;;)
+            {
+                const auto t = Clock::now();
+                if (t >= deadline)
+                    break;
+#if defined(AGS_DISABLE_THREADS)
+                if (t >= next_poll && (deadline - t) > poll_cost + std::chrono::milliseconds(1))
+                {
+                    audio_core_entry_poll();
+                    const auto t_after = Clock::now();
+                    poll_cost = std::max<Clock::duration>(t_after - t, poll_cost - poll_cost / 8);
+                    next_poll = t_after + std::chrono::milliseconds(1);
+                }
+#endif
+            }
+        }
 #else
         std::this_thread::sleep_for(frame_time_remaining);
 #endif
diff --git a/Engine/main/game_run.cpp b/Engine/main/game_run.cpp
index 396d14546..940d024c4 100644
--- a/Engine/main/game_run.cpp
+++ b/Engine/main/game_run.cpp
@@ -1220,6 +1220,11 @@ static void game_loop_update_loop_counter()
     if (play.shakesc_length > 0) play.shakesc_length--;
 }
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+static void dos_fps_sample_at(const Clock::time_point &now);    // patch 0096
+static void dos_memory_sample_at(const Clock::time_point &now); // patch 0096
+#endif
+
 static void game_loop_update_fps()
 {
     const auto now = Clock::now();
@@ -1239,8 +1244,14 @@ static void game_loop_update_fps()
     }
 
 #ifdef __DJGPP__
-    dos_fps_sample_tick();
-    dos_memory_sample_tick();
+    // DOS-PORT patch 0096: both once-a-second samplers gate on the time
+    // this function already read, instead of each reading the clock again
+    // -- three reads per tick became one. (They stay TIME-gated rather
+    // than counting ticks: fps_p50 is a percentile over once-per-real-
+    // second samples, and sampling every N ticks would make it
+    // tick-weighted and incomparable with every figure recorded so far.)
+    dos_fps_sample_at(now);
+    dos_memory_sample_at(now);
     dos_auto_exit_check();
 #endif
 }
@@ -1259,7 +1270,11 @@ static void dos_persec_sample(); // patch 0093, defined with the rest of the yar
 
 void dos_fps_sample_tick()
 {
-    const auto now = Clock::now();
+    dos_fps_sample_at(Clock::now());
+}
+
+static void dos_fps_sample_at(const Clock::time_point &now)
+{
     if (!dos_fps_have_last_sample_time)
     {
         dos_fps_last_sample_time = now;
@@ -1311,7 +1326,11 @@ static long dos_mem_min_free_kb = -1;
 
 void dos_memory_sample_tick()
 {
-    const auto now = Clock::now();
+    dos_memory_sample_at(Clock::now());
+}
+
+static void dos_memory_sample_at(const Clock::time_point &now)
+{
     if (!dos_mem_have_last_sample_time)
     {
         dos_mem_last_sample_time = now;
diff --git a/Engine/util/time_util.h b/Engine/util/time_util.h
index cab21ddc2..b4ca3bc25 100644
--- a/Engine/util/time_util.h
+++ b/Engine/util/time_util.h
@@ -20,12 +20,76 @@
 
 #include <type_traits>
 #include <chrono>
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+#include <time.h> // uclock(), uclock_t, UCLOCKS_PER_SEC -- DOS-PORT patch 0096
+#endif
 
 namespace AGS
 {
 namespace Engine
 {
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+// DOS-PORT patch 0096: on DJGPP every std::chrono clock is gettimeofday()
+// underneath (libstdc++ here is built with _GLIBCXX_USE_GETTIMEOFDAY and
+// WITHOUT _GLIBCXX_USE_CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so steady_clock is not even
+// steady), and gettimeofday() is two real-mode INT 21h round trips plus
+// mktime(): 1324.6 us per read and 55 ms resolution, measured on a
+// 486DX-66 (tests/harness/results/clock-probe/). The engine reads Clock
+// ~4.7 times per game tick, i.e. ~6.3 ms of every 25 ms tick went to
+// asking what time it is, and got an answer good to two frames.
+//
+// uclock() is DJGPP's PIT-backed clock: the BIOS tick count at 0040:006C
+// in the high bits, the live 8254 channel 0 counter in the low 16;
+// 1,193,180 units/s, 4.9 us per read on the same machine. Its real limits,
+// read from libc's own uclock.o, and what each costs us here:
+//  - It CAN step backwards by one BIOS tick (~55 ms): when the counter
+//    has reloaded but IRQ0 has not been serviced yet, the tick count it
+//    pairs the fresh counter value with is stale. This port has already
+//    logged it happening (tickstats clamped_neg). Hence the guard below:
+//    a read below the last returned value returns the last value, so
+//    time stalls for the interrupt-latency window instead of jumping
+//    back. Without the guard is_steady = true would be a lie; with it,
+//    it is not.
+//  - Midnight is handled inside uclock() (one rollover per call pair;
+//    the engine calls it many times a second).
+//  - The first call reprograms PIT channel 0 to mode 2 and blocks until
+//    the next BIOS tick (<= 55 ms). Not new: this port's own
+//    instrumentation has called uclock() since patch 0079.
+//  - It only keeps time while IRQ0 is serviced. Nothing here masks
+//    interrupts; patch 0095's RTC cross-check exists to catch it if
+//    anything ever does.
+//
+// duration is nanoseconds on purpose -- the same type libstdc++'s
+// steady_clock has, so every Clock::duration / duration_cast in the
+// engine compiles to exactly what it did before. The conversion is split
+// into whole seconds and remainder because t * 1e9 overflows int64 after
+// about two hours. The epoch is the first uclock() call; nothing in the
+// engine converts a Clock::time_point to calendar time (that is all on
+// ScriptDateTime::SystemClock, i.e. std::chrono::system_clock).
+struct DosUclockClock
+{
+    typedef std::chrono::nanoseconds duration;
+    typedef duration::rep rep;
+    typedef duration::period period;
+    typedef std::chrono::time_point<DosUclockClock> time_point;
+    static constexpr bool is_steady = true;
+
+    static time_point now() noexcept
+    {
+        static uclock_t last = 0;
+        uclock_t t = uclock();
+        if (t < last)
+            t = last;
+        else
+            last = t;
+        const long long secs = t / UCLOCKS_PER_SEC;
+        const long long rem = t - secs * UCLOCKS_PER_SEC;
+        return time_point(duration(secs * 1000000000LL + rem * 1000000000LL / UCLOCKS_PER_SEC));
+    }
+};
+using Clock = DosUclockClock;
+#else
 // Clock is a high-resolution clock, used when we need max precision.
 // Use high resolution clock only if we know it is monotonic/steady.
 // refer to https://stackoverflow.com/a/38253266/84262
@@ -33,9 +97,22 @@ using Clock = std::conditional<
     std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::is_steady,
     std::chrono::high_resolution_clock, std::chrono::steady_clock
 >::type;
+#endif
 // FastClock is a higher performance clock, used when the speed is a priority,
 // but precision may be sacrificed.
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+// DOS-PORT patch 0096: on DJGPP system_clock is the SAME 1.3 ms
+// gettimeofday() as every other std::chrono clock, so "fast" it is not --
+// and ccInstance::Run() reads it on every script function run (the
+// script-timeout watchdog, cc_instance.cpp), which put a clock read inside
+// every rep_exec call of every tick. All of FastClock's users take
+// differences of two readings (that watchdog; game.cpp's load-time
+// stopwatches); none converts one to calendar time. So it can be the
+// uclock-backed Clock above.
+using FastClock = Clock;
+#else
 using FastClock = std::chrono::system_clock;
+#endif
 
 template <typename TDur>
 inline int64_t ToMilliseconds(TDur dur)
